        "QuestionID": "QID30"
      }
    },
    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID31",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q8d9f7502d4212ba0-chunk_709860c76fcee4886e50b570",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>1.1 threats of harm</strong> – Expressing intent, willingness or desire to harm an individual woman or group of women. This could include, but is not limited to: physical, sexual, emotional or privacy-based forms of harm.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">2. SOCIETAL CONTEXT AND ROOT CAUSES OF CYBER VIOLENCE AND HATE SPEECH ONLINE AGAINST WOMEN > 2.3. The architecture of cyber spaces and women’s victimisation > 2.3.3. Mob mentality<br><br>They include rape and death threats — often credible ones — and frequently involve real-life targeting, which adds to the credibility of the threats;\n\nThey cross multiple social media or online platforms;\n\nThey occur at unusually high levels of intensity and frequency (numerous threats or messages per day or even per hour);\n\nThey are perpetuated for an unusual duration (months or even years);\n\nThey involve many attackers in a concerted and often coordinated campaign.\n\nDanielle Keats Citron also describes how violent mobs threaten and target women with sexual violence, especially women with intersecting vulnerabilities. She explains the role of crowds in destroying privacy</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Q8d9f7502d4212ba0-chunk_709860c76fcee4886e50b570",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
