    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_1X34YGdTiaOe2G2",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID71",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Qff7d86a671c0671f-chunk_502312251b22b86e8485d0a2",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>3.3 backhanded gendered compliments</strong> – Ostensibly complimenting women, but actually belittling or implying their inferiority. This could include, but is not limited to: reduction of women’s value to their attractiveness or sexual desirability, or implication that women are innately frail, helpless or weak.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Impact of sexist hate speech on women and girls<br><br>Sexist hate speech is often treated as a harmless and non-serious issue and women are explicitly or implicitly told to bear with it. However, not only does sexist hate speech undermine freedom of speech for women and girls, but its psychological, emotional and/or physical impacts are real and severe. The aim of sexist hate speech is to humiliate or objectify women, to undervalue their skills and opinions, to destroy their reputation, to make them feel vulnerable and fearful, and to control and punish them for not following a certain behaviour. Sexist hate speech has the effect of silencing women, obliging them to adapt their behaviour and limit their movements and participation in diverse human activities.</div><br>",
        "DataExportTag": "Qff7d86a671c0671f-chunk_502312251b22b86e8485d0a2",
        "QuestionType": "MC",
        "Selector": "SAVR",
        "SubSelector": "TX",
